Enhancement: Back up a very wide directory without holding its tree in memory

A directory's tree is a single blob listing all of its entries, a few hundred
bytes each. restic built that blob in memory, held it while the directory was
walked, and then compressed and encrypted it from that same copy, so backing up a
directory of a million entries needed several hundred megabytes for the tree
alone. On a host that caps the memory a process may use -- some managed WordPress
hosts kill a backup at well under a gigabyte -- that made such a directory
impossible to back up at all.

For a directory of 50000 entries or more the tree is now written to a temporary
file as the directory is walked, then hashed and compressed as it is read back,
so it is never held in one piece. If a temporary file cannot be created, restic
falls back to building the tree in memory as before.

Backing up one directory of a million entries now peaks at 208 MB instead of
1248 MB, and succeeds under a 250 MiB memory cap. The tree written is unchanged.

